hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well one of the topics  concerning friendship that Marcus  Tullius Cicero addresses really on in  his work on friendship is the  relationship between what gets  translated in some of the text as  advantage also as usefulness or utility  and friendship itself and there's a long  back history to this that we should  reflect upon a bit  Aristotle very famously distinguished  three different kinds of friendship one  of which is genuine friendship and it's  friendship in terms of the good as such  or the the noble virtue you get to know  the person and value them for themselves  and then there's friendships of pleasure  and then there's friendships of utility  or usefulness to--some Pharaon in greek  utility toss in latin and so Cicero is  well aware of this set of distinctions  now friendships of utility are  friendships in the weakest sense they  break down quite easily because they're  based on the mutual usefulness of the  people to each other and within the  scope of that relationship they have the  elements of friendship for example  mutual goodwill so long as the  usefulness holds up so the usefulness  is the basis for the friendship not like  friendships where the basis is you  actually liking the person and admiring  them and thinking they're a good person  and wanting to share time and indeed  your life with them so we've got we've  got two different kinds of friendships  and Cicero is less willing than  Aristotle to call friendships of utility  friendships in any real sense they are  definitely relationships but they're  lacking in some of the the key elements  and so there's this long backstory  already philosophically and then there's  all sorts of discussions about real-life  friendships that people are having that  sometimes break down so we can say that  friendship definitely for Cicero is  something that is advantageous the  question is is the basis of an advantage  and Cicero thinks that the basis of it a  genuine friendship is not the  friendships advantageous Nisour  usefulness he does note that there are  quite a few people who do reduce  friendship to advantage or usefulness  and they assume that advantages as he  puts it the cement of friendship as a  matter of fact he frames it in a verbal  way saying that it's what holds that  friendship together nom see you Julie  toss conglutination  is right and then he says 'hey adam  camuto dis all Verret when when that's  gone when that usefulness is taken away  the friendship breaks apart it dissolves  and so you know in this case what we've  got is a double use of the advantageous  the advantage is what sparks the  friendship one person needs something  from another the other person needs  something from them and then they have  the friendship going on so long as what  they're getting what they need the  friendship per ders and once you take  that element away it's gone  Cicero thinks that this is a misguided  view of a friendship that shouldn't be  based on usefulness as a matter of fact  he spends a good bit of time explaining  why real friendship is actually  something that arise  out of nature but he has two other  considerations that are very important  as well that are connected with this  this notion of arising out of nature one  is that people who don't need things  from other people and I think he has in  mind his great friend  Scipio Laelius his friend here very  successful person in in life who who  didn't really need things from other  people but who was very friendly towards  them was helping them out he said well  what advantage is he getting from them  really no advantage she doesn't need  their praise he doesn't need their money  he doesn't need their political offices  he's God at all he just wants to do good  where good can be done and so if  friendship were only based on usefulness  nobody would do that sort of thing right  they would always say well what am I  going to get out of it you're not going  to get anything out of it you're going  to get the opportunity to be a friend  and that's a good thing the other thing  that we want to keep in mind is that for  Cicero there are certain key elements or  dimensions of any real friendship one of  them is affection felt towards the other  person the other is what he calls  goodwill been a volunteer and this is  something that again Aristotle  identified is at the core of real  friendship as well so we feel mutual  goodwill towards others and that's  really what provides the basis of the  friendship not the usefulness the  usefulness is something that comes out  of friendship and you know good  friendship ought to be useful in some  respects but it's not the basis of it  and it's not what holds the friendship  together if it's a genuine friendship  now all that said Cicero is is going to  say that the greatest advantages will be  realized from friendship right so what  are some of these greatest advantages he  actually says friendship offers  advantages almost beyond my power to  describe almost because he is going to  describe them of course  so he says in the first place how can  life be what any is calls the life worth  living if it does not repose on the  mutual goodwill a friend again we see  that been a volunteer goodwill is  absolutely central you can live a life  but how is it going to be the life worth  living  think of the good life without friends  mutual goodwill is part of what makes  life worth living  makes it a decent life and you can think  about the times when you've been  particularly lonely or isolated on your  own don't know anybody separated from  the people that you do know through you  know disagreements or things like that  and how it takes a lot of the joy and  luster out of one's life and I think  he's quite right there he says what is  sweeter than to have someone with whom  you may dare discuss anything as if you  were communing with yourself now that is  a really wonderful phrase there and it  captures something about friendship you  know you can you can have your  co-workers but there's sides of yourself  that you wouldn't reveal to them all  right  some people even don't reveal themselves  to their their lovers their romantic  interests they have their life over here  with them and then they have you know  their friends and their friends actually  know them better we make sitcoms and  movies about this sooner or later the  lover discovers that the friend actually  knows the person better and they feel  you know upset and jealous about this  and a lot of you know plot points and  Sue after that depending on on which  movie we're talking about but there's a  reason for that  genuine intimacy means being able to  share yourself with another person and  there is something provided yourself as  a good self there's something really  good about that even being able to bear  the parts that are bruised and broken  and scarred over and in need of healing  can be incredibly liberating as well so  that that's one side of that he also  says there's  another part in prosperity and in  adversity friends help us out so he says  how could your enjoyment in times of  prosperity be so great if you did 